    {
      "tile_id": "tile_pandas_dataframe_reading",
      "title": "DataFrames, Series, and Reading Data",
      "summary": "Learn the two core pandas data structures — Series and DataFrame — and how to load tabular data from CSV and Excel files into a DataFrame.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "step_id": "tile_pandas_dataframe_reading.s01",
          "type": "explainer",
          "concept_ids": [
            "pandas.dataframe_series_model"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "Series & DataFrame",
          "title": "Two building blocks: Series and DataFrame",
          "lede": "Every pandas table is built from just two structures. Understanding them makes every later operation predictable.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_pandas_dataframe_reading.s01.b1",
              "role": "definition",
              "text": "A pandas **Series** is a one-dimensional labeled array that can hold data of any type. Its axis labels are collectively called the **index**. Think of it as a single column of a spreadsheet: each cell has a value, and each row has a label on the left.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "pandas.dataframe_series_model"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "pandas.dataframe_series_model#tb1"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "pandas.dataframe_series_model#f1"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_pandas_dataframe_reading.s01.b2",
              "role": "example",
              "text": "A Series can be built from a Python list, a dict, or a scalar. `pd.Series([18, 20, 22, 22, 26, 28, 80])` — SwiftDrop delivery times — gets index labels 0 through 6 by default. `pd.Series({\"Lagos\": 18, \"Abuja\": 20, \"Kano\": 22})` uses the dict keys as the index instead.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "pandas.dataframe_series_model"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "pandas.dataframe_series_model#tb2"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "pandas.dataframe_series_model#f3"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_pandas_dataframe_reading.s01.b3",
              "role": "definition",
              "text": "A pandas **DataFrame** is a two-dimensional labeled data structure whose columns can hold different types. It can be thought of as a dict of Series objects that all share the same index — one Series per column, all aligned row-by-row.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "pandas.dataframe_series_model"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "pandas.dataframe_series_model#tb3"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "pandas.dataframe_series_model#f2"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "diagram",
              "block_id": "tile_pandas_dataframe_reading.s01.b4",
              "diagram_kind": "container",
              "title": "A DataFrame is a collection of Series sharing one index",
              "nodes": [
                {
                  "id": "idx",
                  "label": "Index\n0\n1\n2",
                  "kind": "index"
                },
                {
