          ],
          "eyebrow": "Series & DataFrame",
          "title": "Building a DataFrame and accessing columns",
          "lede": "Pass a dict of equal-length lists to pd.DataFrame and use bracket notation to get or set columns.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_pandas_dataframe_reading.s03.b1",
              "role": "example",
              "text": "Pass a dict of equal-length lists to `pd.DataFrame()` and each key becomes a column name: `pd.DataFrame({\"order_id\": [1001, 1002, 1003], \"city\": [\"Lagos\", \"Abuja\", \"Kano\"], \"delivery_time_minutes\": [18, 20, 22]})` produces a three-column, three-row orders table.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "pandas.dataframe_series_model"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "pandas.dataframe_series_model#tb4"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "pandas.dataframe_series_model#f4"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_pandas_dataframe_reading.s03.b2",
              "role": "mechanism",
              "text": "A DataFrame behaves like a dict of like-indexed Series. `orders['city']` returns the city column as a Series. `orders['courier'] = ['Ada', 'Bayo', 'Chidi']` adds a new column — the same bracket syntax you use to read or write a dict entry.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "pandas.dataframe_series_model"
                ],
